###############################################################################
# 07 — Settings, feature flags and the audit log
#
# The audit log is read-only through the API. There is no write endpoint, no
# edit endpoint and no delete endpoint, and the table refuses UPDATE, DELETE and
# TRUNCATE at the database level with no exception for administrators — an
# administrator who can rewrite history can erase their own escalation.
#
# NEVER COMMIT A REAL CREDENTIAL OR TOKEN TO THIS FILE.
###############################################################################

@baseUrl = http://localhost:8090
@accessToken = REPLACE_WITH_ACCESS_TOKEN

@settingKey = registration.mode
@flagKey = client_portal
@auditEventId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-00000000000a
@actorUserId = 00000000-0000-7000-8000-000000000001
@version = 1


# =============================================================================
# Settings
# =============================================================================

### Read every setting
# Correct response: 200 {items: [...]}. A fixed catalogue rather than a
# paginated collection. `is_security_sensitive` marks the settings whose
# modification is itself a security event.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/settings
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Write one setting
# The key comes from the PATH and is not a member of the body: a body that also
# carried the key would let it silently retarget a URL-authorised write.
# `is_security_sensitive`, `value_type` and `description` are likewise not
# writable — being able to re-describe a setting is being able to pick which
# permission guards it.
#
# `version` is mandatory. An absent version would make a blind overwrite the
# default behaviour; a stale one is 409 VERSION_CONFLICT.
#
# Correct response: 200 with the updated setting and an incremented `version`.
# Note: changing `registration.mode` is a security-sensitive write and is
# audited as one.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/settings/{{settingKey}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "value": "INVITE_ONLY",
  "version": {{version}}
}


### Read every feature flag
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/feature-flags
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Turn one feature flag on
# Correct response: 200 with the updated flag. `version` is mandatory for the
# same reason as on settings.
PUT {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/feature-flags/{{flagKey}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json
Accept: application/json

{
  "enabled": true,
  "version": {{version}}
}


# =============================================================================
# Audit
# =============================================================================

### Read the audit log
# Cursor-paginated, newest first by default.
# Correct response: 200 with items carrying seq, id, occurred_at, actor,
# action_code, target, outcome, request_id, source_ip_hint, metadata and the
# chain hashes.
#
# `metadata` is a closed, sanitised structure written by the server — never a
# raw request body. No password, no token of any kind, no TOTP secret, no
# recovery code and no key ever appears in it.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/audit/events?limit=50&direction=desc
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Filter the audit log
# Every filter is optional. `occurred_from` is inclusive and `occurred_to`
# exclusive, both RFC 3339.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/audit/events?limit=50&action_code=ROLE.ASSIGNED&outcome=DENIED&actor_user_id={{actorUserId}}&occurred_from=2026-08-01T00:00:00Z&occurred_to=2026-09-01T00:00:00Z
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Read one audit event
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/audit/events/{{auditEventId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


### Verify the hash chain  — REQUIRES STEP-UP
# Recomputes the HMAC-SHA256 chain over the range and reports the first sequence
# number at which it breaks, if any.
#
# This is a GET behind step-up, which looks odd until you see why: it mutates
# nothing, but it is expensive enough to be a denial-of-service lever, so it is
# gated the same way the dangerous writes are. It is the one deliberate
# exception to "a GET is never behind step-up".
#
# Correct response: 200 {valid: true, checked_from_seq, checked_to_seq,
# events_checked, broken_at_seq: null, verified_at}.
# Without a recent second factor: 403 STEP_UP_REQUIRED.
#
# What a `valid: true` means, precisely: modification, deletion or reordering
# performed WITHOUT the chain key is detectable. It is not a claim of
# tamper-proofing against an adversary who holds both the database and the key.
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/audit/verify?from_seq=1&to_seq=100000
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Accept: application/json


###############################################################################
# Absent by design. Each of these returns 405 (or 404) from the router because
# no such route exists in ROUTE_TABLE — they are not merely forbidden.
###############################################################################

### Writing an audit event
# EXPECTED: 405 / 404. Audit rows are written only by the server as a side
# effect of the operation being audited. There is no API for forging one.
POST {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/audit/events
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "action_code": "NOTHING.HAPPENED",
  "outcome": "SUCCESS"
}


### Deleting an audit event
# EXPECTED: 405 / 404. Even with database access the trigger refuses; even with
# the trigger disabled the runtime role cannot disable it, because it does not
# own the table.
DELETE {{baseUrl}}/api/v1/audit/events/{{auditEventId}}
Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
